Title: "Predicates and Quantifiers"
Id: "70fb4c9d-8dde-4109-a032-6da3247867c1"


Text: "While propositional logic provides the rules for combining complete,
       self-contained assertions, it is blind to the internal anatomy of
       statements. In propositional logic, the statements 'Every continuous
       function on a closed interval is bounded' and '$7$ is a prime number' are
       merely opaque atomic variables $P$ and $Q$. Propositional logic cannot
       express the fact that $7$ possesses a property shared by other numbers,
       nor can it account for the word 'every'.

       To capture the full depth of mathematical reasoning, we must peer inside
       assertions. In 1879, Gottlob Frege revolutionized logic by replacing the
       traditional subject-predicate grammar of classical philosophy with the
       mathematical concept of a *function*. An assertion is understood as a
       property or relation—a *predicate*—applied to one or more mathematical
       objects. By introducing *quantifiers* that govern variables ranging over
       specified domains, we obtain *first-order predicate logic*, the
       expressive medium in which modern mathematics is written."
Id: "3ea75cf2-0c9a-4882-ad3f-ed0c34a3a53c"


SectionTitle: "Predicates, Domains, and Variables"
Id: "76392982-4763-4264-aea1-c7bac3d909fa"


Text: "In contrast to propositional logic, which treats entire statements as
       opaque atoms, mathematical reasoning requires analyzing the internal
       components of an assertion. By viewing mathematical properties as
       functions mapping objects of a domain to truth values, we arrive at the
       foundational concept of a predicate:"
Id: "2498f869-7009-411a-bc01-9a76ffa07912"


Text: "**Definition.** Let $D_1,\ldots,D_n$ be domains. An $n$-ary predicate $P$
       on these domains is an expression for which $P(a_1,\ldots,a_n)$ is a
       statement whenever $a_i\in D_i$ for every $i$. Equivalently, after a
       semantics has been fixed, $P$ determines a truth-valued function on
       $D_1\times\cdots\times D_n$. The domains are part of the specification:
       the same formula may define different predicates when its variables range
       over different objects."
Id: "5425d771-9398-4949-aeac-86991b073f13"


Text: "When an open formula is constructed, the variables appearing within it
       can function in two fundamentally distinct capacities. A variable may
       serve as an open parameter whose value can be varied, or it may be bound
       to a quantifier that limits its scope to an internal dummy index:"
Id: "7820a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d502"


Text: "**Definition.** An occurrence of a variable is *bound* if it lies within
       the scope of a quantifier that binds that variable; otherwise it is
       *free*. A formula with no free variables is a sentence and therefore has
       a truth value in a fixed interpretation. A formula with free variables
       denotes a condition on possible assignments to those variables. Renaming
       a bound variable does not change meaning, provided the new name does not
       capture a formerly free occurrence."
Id: "4d77ab37-a8da-4d6b-b9c7-d5dc9036755d"


Text: "This distinction between free and bound variables is entirely analogous
       to familiar constructs in calculus and linear algebra: the index $i$ in a
       sum $\sum_{i=1}^n a_i$ or the variable $t$ in a definite integral
       $\int_a^b f(t)\,dt$ is bound, while $n$, $a$, and $b$ are free. Renaming
       $t$ to $u$ in $\int_a^b f(u)\,du$ leaves the integral unchanged (a
       property known in logic as *$\alpha$-equivalence*).

       Care is required when substituting expressions into formulas: if a
       substituted term contains a variable that happens to match a quantifier
       inside the formula, accidental *variable capture* can occur, radically
       corrupting the meaning. A rigorous deductive system systematically
       renames bound variables whenever substitution threatens to capture them."
Id: "4289625b-21c1-495f-889f-ea1d4d707fd6"


SectionTitle: "Universal and Existential Quantification"
Id: "dc40bcba-5c18-4126-86c9-56e178a65d78"


Text: "Quantifiers transform open predicates into closed propositions by
       specifying how widely a condition holds across its domain. The most
       demanding condition requires the predicate to be satisfied by every
       member of the domain without exception:"
Id: "64738a4d-451e-9a18-971c-26a578912301"


Text: "**Definition.** For a predicate $P(x)$ on a domain $D$, the universal
       statement $\forall x\in D\,P(x)$ (read *for all $x$ in $D$, $P(x)$*) is
       true exactly when $P(a)$ is true for every single element $a\in D$.

       To prove a universal assertion, one must consider an *arbitrary* element
       $x \in D$ and prove that $P(x)$ holds using only the defining properties
       of $D$, without assuming any special traits of the chosen element.
       Conversely, to refute a universal assertion, one need only exhibit a
       single *counterexample*—an element $c \in D$ for which $P(c)$ fails."
Id: "549d47c5-700f-4cd5-86a2-26533b189178"


Text: "While universal quantification demands that a predicate hold without
       exception across an entire domain, mathematical theorems just as often
       assert the presence of at least one object fulfilling a condition. This
       requirement is formalized by existential quantification:"
Id: "370df2d8-20d4-4f4a-bb3c-0874dc5eb8bd"


Text: "**Definition.** For a predicate $P(x)$ on a domain $D$, the existential
       statement $\exists x\in D\,P(x)$ (read *there exists $x$ in $D$ such that
       $P(x)$*) is true exactly when at least one element $a\in D$ satisfies
       $P(a)$. Such an element is called a *witness*.

       An existential claim can be proved *constructively* by explicitly
       producing a witness and verifying that it satisfies $P$, or
       *non-constructively* by demonstrating that the assumption that no witness
       exists leads to an insurmountable contradiction. Note that an existential
       assertion guarantees only that *at least one* witness exists; it makes no
       claim regarding whether the witness is unique."
Id: "60a3796d-d17b-4e3f-b2a1-1878e1468e61"


Text: "The duality between conjunction and disjunction in propositional logic
       extends directly to quantifiers over infinite domains. A universal claim
       functions as a generalized conjunction indexed across the domain;
       refuting such an assertion requires only exhibiting a single
       counterexample:"
Id: "3748291a-bc01-411a-7009-9a76ffa07999"


Theorem:
given:
. D is \set
. P(x_) is (_ is \\anything) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. not:
  . forAll: x "in" D
    then: P(x)
iff:
. exists: x "in" D
  suchThat:
  . not: P(x)
Documented:
. called: "Negation of a Universal Quantifier"
. description: "De Morgan duality for infinite conjunctions: the negation of a
                universal statement $\neg \forall x \in D \, P(x)$ is logically
                equivalent to the existential assertion of a counterexample
                $\exists x \in D \, \neg P(x)$."
Proof: """By definition, the statement $\forall x \in D, P(x)$ is true if and
          only if $P(x)$ holds for every element $x \in D$. Its negation asserts
          that it is false that every element satisfies $P(x)$. This occurs
          precisely when there is at least one element $x \in D$ for which
          $P(x)$ fails to hold, which is the definition of
          $\exists x \in D, \neg P(x)$."""
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{82}
Id: "bf4df541-b2ff-4fff-9f32-e0565e88a24a"


Text: "Dually, an existential claim behaves as an infinite disjunction. Negating
       an existential claim therefore asserts that no witness exists—which is to
       say, every candidate in the domain fails the condition:"
Id: "0f8d3291-af61-46f0-ad31-dbaba27d7d5d"


Theorem:
given:
. D is \set
. P(x_) is (_ is \\anything) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. not:
  . exists: x "in" D
    suchThat: P(x)
iff:
. forAll: x "in" D
  then:
  . not: P(x)
Documented:
. called: "Negation of an Existential Quantifier"
. description: "De Morgan duality for infinite disjunctions: the negation of an
                existential statement $\neg \exists x \in D \, P(x)$ is
                logically equivalent to the universal exhaustion of failures
                $\forall x \in D \, \neg P(x)$."
Proof: """By definition, the statement $\exists x \in D, P(x)$ is true if and
          only if there exists at least one witness $x \in D$ satisfying $P(x)$.
          The negation asserts that no such witness exists. In other words,
          every candidate $x \in D$ fails to satisfy $P(x)$, which is precisely
          the assertion $\forall x \in D, \neg P(x)$."""
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{82}
Id: "4396c679-4735-45fb-ba6a-704ee55fc63b"


Text: "When multiple quantifiers appear in a single formula, their ordering is
       of paramount mathematical consequence. In predicate calculus, *quantifier
       order records dependency*.

       Consider a binary relation $R(x, y)$ over a domain $D$. The statement:

       $$
       \forall x \, \exists y \, R(x, y)
       $$

       asserts that for each individual
       $x$, there exists a corresponding $y$. Crucially, the choice of $y$ is
       permitted to depend directly upon $x$. By contrast, the statement:

       $$
       \exists y \, \forall x \, R(x, y)
       $$

       demands that a single, universal
       witness $y$ must be chosen *in advance*, functioning simultaneously for
       all possible values of $x$.

       In colloquial terms, 'Everyone has a mother'
       ($\forall x \, \exists y \, M(y, x)$) is an ordinary biological truth;
       but 'There is someone who is the mother of everyone'
       ($\exists y \, \forall x \, M(y, x)$) is an absurdity.

       In higher mathematics, this distinction between $\forall x \exists y$ and
       $\exists y \forall x$ separates elementary properties from deep
       structural ones. In real analysis, it is precisely the distinction
       between *pointwise continuity* (where for every point $x$ and tolerance
       $\varepsilon$ there exists a radius $\delta(x, \varepsilon)$) and
       *uniform continuity* (where a single radius $\delta(\varepsilon)$ must
       work uniformly across the entire domain)."
Id: "f983ba30-e2c8-45f9-afd9-8e19e841e502"


SectionTitle: "Existence and Uniqueness"
Id: "cdaab479-a146-47b9-b147-de10ed82aa04"


Text: "A common mathematical requirement is that an object satisfying a given
       condition is not merely present, but unique. This combined assertion of
       existence and uniqueness is encapsulated by the unique existential
       quantifier:"
Id: "4810a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d503"


Text: "**Definition.** The notation $\exists!x\in D\,P(x)$ asserts existence and
       uniqueness: there is an $a\in D$ satisfying $P(a)$, and any $b\in D$
       satisfying $P(b)$ equals $a$. A proof therefore has two logically
       independent parts. It is often useful to prove uniqueness conditionally
       before constructing an object, because the uniqueness argument identifies
       which properties a successful construction must have."
Id: "730615eb-feb0-45d1-8ca7-82c3138f5eb7"


Text: "The verification of uniqueness is what entitles mathematicians to
       introduce definite names and functional notation. As Bertrand Russell
       observed, an expression such as 'the identity element' or 'the inverse'
       is legitimate only when both existence and uniqueness have been formally
       secured:"
Id: "5912a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d504"


Text: "**Theorem.** If $\exists!x\in D\,P(x)$, then a definition of the form
       'let $a$ be the unique element of $D$ satisfying $P$' is unambiguous.
       Without prior existence and uniqueness, the same phrase does not define
       an object: it merely presupposes one."
Id: "09f10995-86d7-409a-8411-247d4acef1f4"


Text: "Finally, we examine the formal structure of *restricted quantifiers*. When
       we write $\forall x \in D \, P(x)$ or $\exists x \in D \, P(x)$, the domain
       restriction is syntactic sugar whose underlying expansion uses the standard
       Boolean connectives:

       $$
       \forall x \in D \, P(x) \quad \equiv \quad \forall x \, (x \in D \Rightarrow P(x))
       $$

       $$
       \exists x \in D \, P(x) \quad \equiv \quad \exists x \, (x \in D \land P(x))
       $$

       The change from conditional ($\Rightarrow$) in the universal quantifier to
       conjunction ($\land$) in the existential quantifier is of the utmost logical
       importance. If one mistakenly wrote $\exists x \, (x \in D \Rightarrow P(x))$,
       the assertion would become trivially true in any universe containing at least
       one object outside $D$! For any element $c \notin D$, the antecedent $c \in D$
       is false, making the conditional $c \in D \Rightarrow P(c)$ vacuously true,
       thereby providing a false witness for the existential claim. Conjunction
       ensures that an existential witness must legitimately reside within the
       intended domain $D$."
Id: "64c6c4ea-6a77-413f-9dda-b1d4bb102443"
